/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Ancient & Medieval batch Y */
WB.L("pompeii-herculaneum", "ancient", "beginner", "Pompeii and Herculaneum: towns frozen in time",
  "In AD 79 a volcano buried two Roman towns in a matter of hours. What it destroyed, it also preserved: bread in the oven, graffiti on the walls and the last moments of the people who lived there.",
  [
    ["A mountain nobody feared", "The people of Pompeii did not recognise Vesuvius as a volcano, so the AD 79 eruption caught them unprepared.",
      "Picture a busy market town on the Bay of Naples, vineyards climbing the slopes of a green mountain behind it. To the Romans, Vesuvius was good farming land. It had not erupted in living memory. Pompeii had perhaps 10,000 to 12,000 residents, while nearby Herculaneum was a smaller, wealthier seaside town. In AD 62 a strong earthquake shook the region, and some buildings were still being repaired when Vesuvius erupted. Nobody connected the tremors with what was coming. The traditional date is 24 August, but clues such as autumn fruit and a charcoal inscription suggest it may have been October. That ignorance is why the eruption was so deadly, and why so much was left exactly where it stood."],
    ["The day the sky fell", "The eruption came in two stages, falling pumice and then deadly pyroclastic surges, and one teenager recorded it.",
      "Around midday, Vesuvius blasted a column of ash and pumice skyward. Pliny the Younger, about 17 and watching from Misenum across the bay, compared its shape to an umbrella pine. For hours pumice rained on Pompeii, collapsing many roofs. Overnight and into the next morning, the column collapsed and sent pyroclastic surges down the mountain: avalanches of superheated gas and ash faster than anyone could run. Herculaneum, closer to the volcano but upwind of the pumice, was hit by these surges and buried under metres of material that hardened into rock.\n\nPliny's two letters to the historian Tacitus are our only surviving eyewitness record. They describe darkness 'like a sealed room'. His uncle, Pliny the Elder, commander of the Roman fleet, died at Stabiae."],
    ["Bodies in the ash", "Plaster poured into hollows left in hardened ash revealed the final postures of the victims.",
      "As the ash around Pompeii's victims hardened, their bodies decayed, leaving hollow spaces shaped exactly like the people who had been there. In 1863, the archaeologist Giuseppe Fiorelli had the idea of pouring liquid plaster into these cavities. When the plaster set and the ash was chipped away, the casts showed people curled up, shielding their faces, or holding one another. Even a dog straining at its chain was preserved. Herculaneum told a different story. For centuries experts assumed most people had escaped, until hundreds of skeletons were found in the 1980s, crowded into boathouses by the old shoreline, apparently waiting for rescue by sea. Their bones now reveal Roman health, diet and injuries."],
    ["Graffiti and takeaway", "Pompeii shows ordinary Roman life, from street-food counters to election posters and scratched graffiti.",
      "Walk down a Pompeii street today and you pass stepping stones for crossing roads, ruts worn by cart wheels and dozens of street-food counters called thermopolia, with big jars set into stone benches. Many Romans lived in small apartments and did not cook at home, so a hot meal on the street was normal. The walls carry painted election notices urging votes for local candidates, and thousands of scratched graffiti: jokes, insults, love notes, boasts and records of debts. Bakeries hold millstones, ovens and carbonised loaves. Grand homes like the House of the Vettii have colourful frescoes, and one doorway mosaic shows a fierce dog with the words 'Cave canem', meaning 'beware of the dog'."],
    ["Reading the unreadable", "Modern X-ray scanning and machine learning are revealing text from Herculaneum scrolls burned into lumps.",
      "After the eruption the towns were forgotten, buried under new soil and later villages. Formal excavations began at Herculaneum in 1738 and at Pompeii in 1748. Early digging was more treasure hunt than science, but methods improved. A luxurious villa at Herculaneum held a library of papyrus scrolls, turned into fragile black lumps by the heat, and attempts to unroll them destroyed many. Recently, researchers have scanned scrolls with powerful X-rays and used machine learning to spot faint traces of ink. In 2023 and 2024, teams revealed passages of Greek philosophy unread for nearly 2,000 years.\n\nExcavation continues, and tourism and weather threaten the ruins. Vesuvius remains active, with hundreds of thousands living nearby, so Italy monitors it and maintains evacuation plans."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why was Pompeii so unprepared for the eruption?", ["The town had no warning from any earlier earthquake","Its residents were all away at the coast","They did not recognise Vesuvius as a volcano or link the tremors to danger","A flood had already damaged the town"], 2, "Nobody connected the earlier earthquake and tremors with the mountain, so few expected an eruption."],
    [1, "What killed most people who stayed in Pompeii?", ["Lava flows","Pyroclastic surges of superheated gas and ash","A tsunami","Poisoned water"], 1, "Fast-moving pyroclastic surges swept through the towns overnight, faster than anyone could run."],
    [2, "How were the famous casts of Pompeii's victims made?", ["Plaster was poured into hollows left where bodies had decayed in hardened ash","Sculptors copied the bodies before the eruption","Lava cooled around the bodies","Ash was carved by hand into figures"], 0, "Hardened ash kept the shape of each body, and liquid plaster filled the hollow to make a cast."],
    [3, "Why were street-food counters so common in Pompeii?", ["Cooking at home was against the law","Many people lived in small apartments and did not cook at home","Food was free from the government","Tourists demanded local dishes"], 1, "With limited cooking space at home, grabbing a hot meal from a counter was an everyday habit."],
    [4, "How are researchers reading the burned Herculaneum scrolls?", ["By soaking them in water","By carefully peeling them by hand","By testing them with chemical dyes","By scanning them with X-rays and using machine learning to find ink"], 3, "X-ray scans combined with machine learning reveal ink inside scrolls that cannot be unrolled."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Bread in the oven, graffiti on the walls. What Pompeii kept after AD 79."});

WB.L("mongol-empire", "ancient", "beginner", "Genghis Khan and the Mongol Empire",
  "A boy abandoned on the harsh steppe grew up to build the largest connected land empire in history. Here is how the Mongols conquered so much, so quickly, and what their rule left behind.",
  [
    ["Outcast to conqueror", "Genghis Khan rose from a hunted outsider by rewarding loyalty and building a disciplined, mixed-tribe army.",
      "Born Temüjin around 1162 on the Mongolian steppe, the future Genghis Khan lost his father to poisoning by a rival tribe, and his clan abandoned the family. They survived on roots and fish at the bottom of steppe society, and he was later captured but escaped. He learned that loyalty mattered more than birth and gathered followers by rewarding those who stood by him. By 1206 he had united the Mongol and Turkic tribes and was proclaimed Genghis Khan.\n\nEvery Mongol man was a skilled horse archer. Genghis grouped them in units of ten, a hundred, a thousand and ten thousand, mixing tribes so loyalty shifted to the khan. Each warrior carried several horses, used feigned retreats, and the army recruited Chinese and Persian engineers to build siege engines."],
    ["Conquest and terror", "The Mongol conquests caused enormous death and destruction, and terror was a deliberate tool.",
      "It would be wrong to romanticise the Mongols. Cities that surrendered were often spared, but those that resisted could be destroyed and their people killed. The Mongols spread stories of these massacres to frighten others into giving up. After the ruler of the Khwarazmian Empire in Central Asia killed Mongol envoys, Genghis Khan's armies devastated the region's great cities from 1219. In 1258 his grandson Hulegu captured Baghdad, then one of the world's great centres of learning, and much of it was sacked. Historians debate the numbers, which ancient writers may have exaggerated, but millions died across Asia and Eastern Europe, and some regions took centuries to recover."],
    ["Empire, split and limits", "The largest connected land empire split into four states and was never invincible.",
      "Genghis Khan died in 1227, but his sons and grandsons kept expanding. Under Ögedei, Mongol armies swept into Russia, Poland and Hungary. In 1242 they withdrew from Europe, which many historians link to news of Ögedei's death requiring the princes to choose a new leader. At its peak in the later 1200s, the realm was the largest connected land empire ever. It soon split into four states: the Golden Horde in Russia, the Ilkhanate in Persia, the Chagatai Khanate in Central Asia, and the Yuan dynasty in China, founded by Kublai Khan.\n\nThe Mongols could be beaten. The Mamluks defeated them at Ain Jalut in 1260, and Kublai's invasions of Japan in 1274 and 1281 failed, partly due to storms. The Ming drove the Yuan from China in 1368."],
    ["Horses, passports and post", "The Mongols held their huge empire together with a relay system, travel passes and written administration.",
      "Ruling such a vast area needed fast communication. The Mongols built the yam, a network of relay stations where official riders could swap horses, get food and rest. Messages could travel remarkable distances in days rather than weeks. Important travellers carried a paiza, a metal or wooden tablet that worked a little like a passport, showing they were under the khan's protection and entitled to help. The Mongols also took censuses, collected taxes and wrote laws. Genghis Khan had his people adopt a script borrowed from the Uyghurs so Mongolian could be written down. For a nomadic people with no tradition of cities, this was a remarkable feat of administration."],
    ["The Mongol Peace", "Unified Mongol rule made Eurasian trade safer for a time, but also helped diseases spread.",
      "Once fighting died down in many regions, the empire's size had an unexpected effect. One family controlled the routes from China to the Middle East and Russia, so merchants could travel the Silk Roads more safely. Historians call this period the Pax Mongolica, or Mongol Peace. Goods, ideas and people moved across Eurasia. Paper money, gunpowder and printing techniques travelled west, while Persian astronomers and doctors worked in China. The Mongols were generally tolerant of different religions, and Buddhists, Muslims, Christians and Daoists could be found at the khan's court. The darker side is that diseases travelled too, and many historians think the Black Death spread along these same routes. Today Genghis Khan is honoured in Mongolia as a national founder, on banknotes and in a giant statue near the capital."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Genghis Khan mix men from different tribes in his army units?", ["To teach them new languages","Because some tribes had no horses","To replace tribal loyalty with loyalty to the khan","To make each unit smaller"], 2, "Mixing tribes broke down old rivalries and bound soldiers to the khan."],
    [1, "Why did the Mongols spread stories of massacres?", ["To frighten other cities into surrendering","To record their history accurately","To attract new settlers","To honour their fallen soldiers"], 0, "Terror was a deliberate tool: fear of destruction pushed cities to give up without a fight."],
    [2, "What happened to the Mongol Empire after its great expansion?", ["It stayed united under one khan for centuries","It split into four large states","It was conquered all at once by the Ming","It moved its capital to Europe"], 1, "The empire divided into the Golden Horde, the Ilkhanate, the Chagatai Khanate and the Yuan dynasty."],
    [3, "What was the Mongol 'yam'?", ["A type of bow","A tax on grain","A religious festival","A network of relay stations where riders swapped horses"], 3, "The yam let messengers change horses, eat and rest, so news travelled fast across the empire."],
    [4, "What was a darker side of the connectedness of the Mongol Peace?", ["Trade routes were closed to merchants","Diseases could travel along the same routes","Religions were banned at court","Paper money was banned"], 1, "Open routes carried goods and ideas, but many historians think they also carried the Black Death."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"He began as a hunted outcast. How did one boy build the largest land empire?"});

WB.L("mali-empire-mansa-musa", "ancient", "beginner", "Mansa Musa and the golden empire of Mali",
  "In 1324 a West African king travelled to Mecca with so much gold that he reportedly upset the economy of Cairo. His empire of Mali was one of the richest in the medieval world.",
  [
    ["A king who dazzled Cairo", "Mansa Musa's 1324 pilgrimage made Mali famous far beyond Africa, even if the details have grown with retelling.",
      "In 1324, a vast caravan arrived in Cairo on its way to Mecca. At its head was Mansa Musa, ruler of the Mali Empire, travelling with thousands of attendants and camels reportedly loaded with gold. The Arab writer al-Umari, who spoke to people in Cairo who had met the king, reported that Musa gave away and spent so much gold that its value in the city fell and had not recovered years later. Exact figures from the time are impossible to check, and later retellings have grown with each telling. But the story spread across the Mediterranean world and put Mali firmly on the mental map of merchants and rulers who had barely heard of West Africa."],
    ["Founders, rulers and griots", "Mali grew from Sundiata Keita's legendary state into an organised empire whose history was preserved by griots.",
      "The Mali Empire grew from a small Mande kingdom on the upper Niger River. Its founder, Sundiata Keita, is remembered in an epic still performed today. In the story he is born unable to walk, mocked and exiled, but returns to defeat the sorcerer king Sumanguru, usually dated to around the 1230s. Historians treat the epic as part history, part legend, but agree Sundiata built a strong state that his successors expanded across much of West Africa.\n\nMali was a patchwork of provinces and tributary kingdoms ruled by a mansa. Local rulers kept their positions if they stayed loyal and paid tribute. Islam was the faith of the court and many merchants, while many ordinary people kept older beliefs. Griots, storytellers and musicians, memorised royal genealogies and history, which is how the Sundiata epic survived."],
    ["Gold and salt", "Mali grew rich by controlling and taxing the trade in West African gold and Saharan salt.",
      "For centuries, much of the gold used in the Mediterranean world and the Middle East came from West Africa. Rich goldfields lay in regions such as Bambuk and Bure, within or near Mali's lands. Mali did not need to mine it all: it controlled and taxed the trade. In the other direction came salt, dug in slabs from desert mines such as Taghaza in the Sahara. In hot climates people need salt to survive, so it was extremely valuable, at times traded weight for weight against gold, according to some accounts. Camel caravans crossed the desert on journeys that could take months, and cities on the edge of the Sahara grew wealthy as meeting points for desert and river trade. Farming along the Niger fed the population, and the river served as a highway."],
    ["Timbuktu, city of books", "Under Mali, Timbuktu became a famous centre of Islamic learning and handwritten manuscripts.",
      "Today 'Timbuktu' is used in English to mean the remotest place imaginable, but in the 1300s it was a thriving crossroads. After returning from Mecca, Mansa Musa is said to have brought scholars and an architect back with him, and the Djinguereyber Mosque, built of mud brick and wood, is traditionally linked to his reign. The city became a centre where scholars taught law, astronomy, mathematics and medicine, and families collected manuscripts over generations. Many of these handwritten books survived in private libraries for centuries. When armed groups occupied Timbuktu in 2012, librarians and residents quietly smuggled hundreds of thousands of manuscripts out of the city in boxes and sacks to keep them safe."],
    ["A traveller's report and decline", "Ibn Battuta described a safe, orderly Mali in the 1350s, but disputes and the rising Songhay Empire later reduced it.",
      "The Moroccan traveller Ibn Battuta visited Mali around 1352 to 1353, some fifteen years after Mansa Musa's death. He described the court of Musa's brother, Mansa Sulayman, with its ceremonies, interpreters and guards. He grumbled that the king's gift to him was disappointingly small, but praised the safety of the roads and noted how seriously people took Friday prayers and their children's learning of the Quran.\n\nAfter Mansa Musa, Mali faced succession disputes, provincial rebellions and desert raids. In the 1400s the Songhay Empire, based at Gao, took Timbuktu and much of the trade. Yet Mali's legacy endured. The Catalan Atlas (around 1375) shows a crowned African ruler holding a gold nugget. Modern Mali took its name from the empire when it became independent from France in 1960, and griots still sing Sundiata's epic."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Mansa Musa's pilgrimage matter for Mali's reputation?", ["It opened Mali's first trade routes","It ended a war with Egypt","It put Mali on the mental map of merchants and rulers across the Mediterranean","It proved Mali was the largest empire in the world"], 2, "The story of his gold spread widely, making Mali famous even though its exact details can't be checked."],
    [1, "What role did griots play in Mali?", ["Tax collectors","Storytellers who preserved history and genealogies in song and speech","Royal bodyguards","Caravan leaders"], 1, "Griots memorised and performed history, which is how the Sundiata epic survived."],
    [2, "How did Mali grow rich from gold without mining all of it?", ["It controlled and taxed the trade","It printed its own money","It captured Egyptian mines","It sold salt to Egypt for silver"], 0, "Mali taxed trade between West African gold and Saharan salt, and caravan cities prospered."],
    [3, "What made Timbuktu famous in the 1300s?", ["Its position as the remotest place on Earth","Its huge standing army","Its role as a centre of learning with collections of manuscripts","Its role as a port on the Atlantic"], 2, "Scholars taught law, astronomy, mathematics and medicine, and families collected manuscripts over generations."],
    [4, "What contributed to Mali's decline after Mansa Musa?", ["A lack of gold in West Africa","Succession disputes, rebellions and the rise of the Songhay Empire","Conquest by France","A ban on trade across the Sahara"], 1, "Internal disputes and the rising Songhay Empire shrank Mali to a smaller kingdom."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"One king's gold trip reportedly shook Cairo's economy. Who was he?"});

WB.L("ancient-olympics", "ancient", "beginner", "The original Olympic Games",
  "For more than a thousand years, Greeks gathered at Olympia to run, wrestle and race chariots in honour of Zeus. The ancient Games were holier, rougher and stranger than the modern version.",
  [
    ["A festival and a truce", "The ancient Olympics were a religious festival for Zeus, protected by a sacred truce that let people travel safely.",
      "Tradition dates the first Olympics to 776 BC, held every four years at Olympia in honour of Zeus. The four-year gap, an Olympiad, became how Greeks counted years. The sanctuary held temples and treasuries and a huge gold and ivory statue of Zeus by Phidias, later counted among the Seven Wonders. On the middle day, priests sacrificed a hundred oxen and the meat fed the crowds.\n\nGreece was hundreds of city-states, often at war. Messengers announced a sacred truce, the ekecheiria, so athletes, trainers and spectators could travel safely and armies stayed out of Elis, which ran Olympia. It did not stop wars and was sometimes broken: Sparta was banned and fined in 420 BC. The modern Olympic Truce echoes the idea."],
    ["Running the stadion", "The first and most prestigious event was the stadion, a sprint of one track length, which gave us the word 'stadium'.",
      "Tradition says the earliest Olympics had just one event: the stadion, a footrace of one length of the track, roughly 190 metres. The winner gave his name to that Olympiad, so being the fastest man in Greece brought fame for generations. Our word 'stadium' comes from this race. Over time came longer foot races, wrestling, boxing, the pentathlon, chariot and horse racing, and a race run in armour. The pentathlon combined running, wrestling, discus, javelin and long jump, and long jumpers swung stone or metal weights called halteres to carry themselves further. You can still walk through the stone tunnel into the ancient stadium at Olympia and stand on the starting line."],
    ["Naked, oiled and fierce", "Athletes competed naked, and combat sports such as the pankration were brutal and sometimes deadly.",
      "Male athletes competed naked, their bodies rubbed with olive oil. The Greeks admired the trained male body, and nudity in sport was part of their culture. The combat events were tough. Boxers wrapped their hands in leather straps rather than padded gloves, and bouts continued until one man gave up or could not go on. The most dangerous event was the pankration, a mix of boxing and wrestling with very few rules. Biting and eye-gouging were banned, but almost everything else was allowed, including strangleholds and bending fingers. Fighters sometimes died. Champions such as the wrestler Milo of Croton, said to have won at six Olympics, became legends whose stories were retold for centuries."],
    ["Women and the Games", "Married women were barred from the Games, but a loophole in chariot racing let Kyniska become an Olympic victor.",
      "According to the ancient writer Pausanias, married women were not allowed to watch the Games, although unmarried girls may have been. Women could not compete in the main events. Yet there was a loophole. In chariot racing, victory went to the horses' owner, not the driver. The Spartan princess Kyniska bred and trained horses, and her team won the four-horse chariot race, probably in 396 BC and again in 392 BC. She became the first woman named an Olympic victor, and a statue of her stood at Olympia. Separately, a festival called the Heraia, in honour of the goddess Hera, included footraces for unmarried young women at Olympia itself."],
    ["Wreaths, rewards and the end", "The official prize was an olive wreath, but fame brought riches and cheats were publicly shamed before the Games faded and were revived in 1896.",
      "An Olympic winner received a crown of wild olive leaves from a sacred tree near the temple of Zeus. There were no silver or bronze medals; only winning counted. Victors returned home as heroes, with cash, free meals for life and statues, and poets such as Pindar wrote victory odes. Athletes caught bribing opponents were fined, and the money paid for bronze statues of Zeus, called Zanes, lining the path into the stadium, their bases naming the offenders.\n\nNero entered a chariot race in AD 67, fell from his chariot and was still declared winner. As Christianity became official, pagan festivals were suppressed, and the Games ended in the late 300s or 400s, though the date is debated. Olympia was later buried and uncovered in the 1800s. The first modern Olympics were held in Athens in 1896."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the purpose of the sacred truce?", ["To let athletes and spectators travel safely to Olympia","To end all wars between Greek city-states permanently","To ban Sparta from the Games","To decide who would host the next Games"], 0, "Messengers announced the truce so people could reach Olympia safely, although it did not stop wars in general."],
    [1, "Why was winning the stadion so prestigious?", ["It paid the highest cash prize in Greece","The winner gave his name to that Olympiad","It was the only event open to the public","It was the longest event"], 1, "The fastest man in Greece lent his name to the whole four-year period, so his fame lasted for generations."],
    [2, "What was the pankration?", ["A relay race","A music competition","A combat sport mixing boxing and wrestling with very few rules","A swimming race"], 2, "The pankration combined boxing and wrestling, banning only biting and eye-gouging, and fighters sometimes died."],
    [3, "How did Kyniska become an Olympic victor?", ["She disguised herself as a man","She won the women's footrace","She was given an honorary title","She owned the winning chariot team"], 3, "Victory in chariot racing went to the owner, so Kyniska's horses won her the title."],
    [4, "What happened to athletes caught cheating by bribery?", ["They were fined, and the money paid for statues naming them","They were banned for life and forgotten","They were forced to return their wreaths","They were exiled from Greece"], 0, "Fines paid for the Zanes, bronze statues of Zeus whose bases publicly named the offenders."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Ancient Olympic winners got a wreath, yet a woman won without competing. How?"});

WB.L("byzantine-empire", "ancient", "intermediate", "Byzantium: the Roman Empire that didn't fall",
  "When Rome fell in the West, its eastern half carried on for almost another thousand years. From its glittering capital Constantinople, the Byzantine Empire kept Roman law, Greek learning and Christian art alive.",
  [
    ["Rome's other half", "The eastern Roman Empire survived until 1453, and its well-placed, heavily walled capital helped it endure.",
      "Most of us learn that the Roman Empire fell in AD 476, when the last emperor in the West was deposed. But the eastern half carried on for nearly another thousand years. Its people called themselves Romans, and its emperors saw themselves as heirs of Augustus. 'Byzantine' was popularised by European scholars long after the empire ended, taken from Byzantium, the old Greek town on whose site the capital was built. Greek replaced Latin as its main language, but its rulers never stopped thinking of themselves as Roman.\n\nIn AD 330 Constantine dedicated Constantinople (now Istanbul) on the Bosporus, controlling the sea route between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean. Water guarded three sides, and Theodosius II's massive land walls guarded the fourth."],
    ["Justinian, Theodora and the law", "Justinian and Theodora reshaped the empire, leaving Hagia Sophia and a legal code that shaped civil law.",
      "Justinian, who ruled from 527 to 565, wanted to restore Rome's glory. His generals reconquered North Africa, Italy and part of Spain, although holding them proved costly. His wife Theodora, once an actress, a low-status job, became one of the most powerful women in Byzantine history. During the Nika riots of 532, the historian Procopius records that she persuaded a wavering Justinian to stay and fight. A terrible plague from 541 later killed a large share of the population.\n\nAfter the riots, Justinian rebuilt Hagia Sophia (537) in about five years. For a thousand years it was the world's largest cathedral. It later became a mosque, a museum, and in 2020 a mosque again. His Corpus Juris Civilis gathered Roman law into one collection and later shaped civil law systems."],
    ["Greek fire and gold coins", "Secret weapons, a stable gold coin and clever diplomacy helped Byzantium survive.",
      "In the late 600s, Arab fleets besieged Constantinople. The Byzantines fought back with Greek fire, a burning liquid sprayed through tubes from ships that kept burning even on water. Its recipe was a closely guarded state secret, so well guarded that we still do not know exactly what was in it. Money mattered as much as weapons. The gold solidus kept a stable gold content for several centuries, and merchants from Europe to Asia trusted it. The empire also used diplomacy cleverly, paying neighbours, arranging marriages and setting enemies against each other. Its survival owed as much to gold and cunning as to soldiers."],
    ["Faith, art and alphabets", "Byzantine Christianity shaped a divided church and, through Cyril and Methodius, the Cyrillic script.",
      "Religion was at the heart of Byzantine life. Theological debates could spark riots, and arguments over religious images, known as iconoclasm, divided the empire in the 700s and 800s. Differences grew between the Church in Constantinople and the Church in Rome, and in 1054 a formal split, the Great Schism, separated what became the Orthodox and Catholic churches. In the 800s, two brothers from Thessaloniki, Cyril and Methodius, were sent as missionaries to the Slavs. They created an alphabet for Slavic languages, and their followers developed the Cyrillic script, named for Cyril. Today it is used for Russian, Ukrainian, Bulgarian, Serbian and other languages, a living legacy of Byzantium across Eastern Europe."],
    ["The long fall", "The Fourth Crusade's 1204 sack crippled Byzantium, and the Ottomans finished it in 1453.",
      "The empire's greatest wound came from fellow Christians. In 1204, knights of the Fourth Crusade, supposedly bound for the Holy Land, sacked Constantinople instead and looted its treasures. The Byzantines recaptured their city in 1261, but the empire never recovered its strength. Meanwhile the Ottoman Turks expanded steadily, until by the 1400s the empire was little more than the capital itself. In 1453, Sultan Mehmed II besieged Constantinople with a huge army and powerful cannons, and on 29 May the city fell. The last emperor, Constantine XI, died in the fighting. Greek scholars who fled to Italy brought manuscripts with them, helping to fuel the Renaissance and carrying ancient Greek learning to the modern world."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the Byzantines consider themselves?", ["Greeks, separate from Rome","Romans, heirs of the Roman Empire","Followers of a new Constantinian empire","Citizens of the old town of Byzantium only"], 1, "They called themselves Romans, and 'Byzantine' was a label popularised by scholars centuries later."],
    [1, "What was the Corpus Juris Civilis?", ["A Byzantine war manual","A book of church hymns","A collection of Roman laws that later shaped civil law systems","A list of emperors"], 2, "Justinian's legal collection organised centuries of Roman law and shaped later European legal systems."],
    [2, "Besides soldiers, what helped Byzantium survive?", ["A stable gold coin and clever diplomacy","Huge taxes on its neighbours","A ban on foreign trade","Peace treaties with every enemy"], 0, "The trusted solidus and diplomacy, along with Greek fire, let Byzantium outlast many enemies."],
    [3, "What is the Cyrillic script named after?", ["A Byzantine emperor","Cyril, a missionary to the Slavs","A Russian city","A Greek goddess"], 1, "Cyril and Methodius created a Slavic alphabet, and their followers developed the script named for Cyril."],
    [4, "What hurt the Byzantine Empire most before the Ottoman conquest?", ["A long plague in the 1400s","The loss of its gold coins","Its own decision to give up the capital","The sack of Constantinople by the Fourth Crusade in 1204"], 3, "Crusaders looted the city in 1204, and the empire never recovered its former strength."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Rome fell in 476, yet Romans ruled from Constantinople until 1453. How?"});

WB.L("persian-empire", "ancient", "intermediate", "Persia: the first superpower",
  "Long before Rome, a Persian king built an empire stretching from Egypt to India. The Achaemenid Persians ran it with roads, couriers, coins and a surprising degree of tolerance.",
  [
    ["Cyrus and his Cylinder", "Cyrus built a vast empire in one lifetime and presented himself as a liberator, a policy of working with local customs.",
      "Around 550 BC, Cyrus, from Persis in what is now south-western Iran, overthrew the Median kingdom that had dominated his people. Within about twenty years he conquered Lydia in Asia Minor, the Greek cities on its coast, and in 539 BC Babylon. His dynasty is the Achaemenids. His son Cambyses added Egypt, and Darius pushed east into the Indus Valley and west into Europe. At its height it covered more land than any empire before it.\n\nThe Cyrus Cylinder, a 23-centimetre clay barrel found at Babylon in 1879, records that Cyrus restored temples and let displaced peoples return home. The Hebrew Bible also praises him for letting Jewish exiles rebuild their temple. Calling it the first human rights charter goes too far: it is royal propaganda. But it reflects a real policy of working with local customs."],
    ["Darius organises an empire", "Darius divided the empire into provinces under governors, checked by inspectors, and standardised coins and language.",
      "Darius I, who ruled from 522 to 486 BC, was the great organiser. He divided the empire into provinces called satrapies, each run by a governor known as a satrap. Satraps collected taxes and kept order, but the king also sent inspectors, whom the Greeks nicknamed 'the eyes and ears of the king', to check on them. Darius introduced a gold coin, the daric, and standardised weights and measures. Officials often wrote in Aramaic, which became a common administrative language across the Near East. Darius also recorded his rise to power on a huge rock carving at Behistun in western Iran, in three languages. Centuries later it helped scholars such as Henry Rawlinson crack cuneiform."],
    ["Royal Road and Persepolis", "A highway with relay stations carried messages across the empire, while Persepolis showed its many peoples in peaceful order.",
      "To hold the realm together, the Persians needed fast communication. The Royal Road ran roughly 2,500 kilometres from Sardis, near the Aegean, to Susa. Stations along it gave official messengers fresh horses, food and rest. Herodotus wrote that nothing, not snow, rain, heat or darkness, stopped these couriers. A version of his words is carved on New York City's main post office. A journey of three months on foot could reportedly be relayed in about a week.\n\nAt Persepolis, Darius built a palace complex whose Apadana hall had tall stone columns topped with carved animals. Carvings show delegates from across the empire bringing gifts, each in their own clothing. Rather than slaughter, the art shows peaceful order, many peoples under one ruler. Clay tablets record rations and wages for workers, including some women."],
    ["Faith and fire", "Zoroastrianism shaped Persian ideas of truth against the Lie, with fire as a symbol of purity.",
      "Many Persian kings honoured Ahura Mazda, the wise lord at the centre of Zoroastrianism, one of the world's oldest surviving religions. Its prophet, Zarathustra, known to the Greeks as Zoroaster, taught of a struggle between truth and order on one side and the lie and chaos on the other. Darius's inscriptions repeatedly attack 'the Lie' and present the king as its enemy. Fire was a sacred symbol of purity, a tradition still important in Zoroastrian fire temples today. Scholars debate how the kings' beliefs related to later Zoroastrian practice. Some ideas, such as a final judgement and a struggle between good and evil, may have influenced later religions, though that too is debated."],
    ["Greek wars and the end", "Our image of Persia comes largely from its Greek enemies, though it stayed a great power until Alexander conquered it.",
      "Much of what Westerners learned about Persia came from its enemies. Darius and his son Xerxes invaded Greece in 490 and 480 BC and were defeated at battles such as Marathon and Salamis. Greek writers portrayed the Persians as luxurious and tyrannical, a picture that coloured European views for centuries. In reality, Persia remained a great power for another 150 years. Its end came when Alexander the Great defeated Darius III in a series of battles and took the empire. In 330 BC Persepolis was burned, whether by accident, revenge or celebration is still argued. Yet Alexander adopted many Persian ways of ruling, and later Iranian empires, the Parthians and Sasanians, would rise again."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How do historians generally view the claim that the Cyrus Cylinder was the first human rights charter?", ["As fully accurate","As a modern forgery","As an exaggeration of what is really royal propaganda","As a Greek invention"], 2, "Historians see it as royal propaganda, though it reflects a real policy of working with local customs."],
    [1, "What were satraps?", ["Persian priests","Provincial governors","Elite soldiers","Royal couriers"], 1, "Satraps governed provinces called satrapies, and royal inspectors checked on them."],
    [2, "Why did the Persians build the Royal Road with stations along it?", ["To let messengers get fresh horses and relay messages quickly","To tax travellers at every stop","To keep foreign traders out","To connect Persepolis to Athens"], 0, "Relay stations let couriers swap horses and rest, holding the huge empire together with fast communication."],
    [3, "How did Darius's inscriptions present the king?", ["As a god on Earth","As an enemy of the Lie","As a servant of the Greeks","As a simple farmer"], 1, "They repeatedly attack 'the Lie' and cast the king as its enemy, reflecting Persian ideas of truth and order."],
    [4, "Why does the Western image of Persia often seem unflattering?", ["Persia never fought anyone","Persian writers described themselves harshly","Much of it comes from Greek enemies who called Persians luxurious and tyrannical","Persia left no records at all"], 2, "Greek writers shaped European views of Persia for centuries, though in reality it stayed a great power for 150 years more."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Persia ran an empire from Egypt to India with roads and couriers. How?"});

WB.L("bronze-age-collapse", "ancient", "advanced", "The Bronze Age collapse",
  "Around 1200 BC, a connected world of kingdoms around the eastern Mediterranean fell apart within a few generations. Palaces burned, writing vanished in places, and historians still debate why.",
  [
    ["A connected ancient world", "Around 1300 BC the eastern Mediterranean kingdoms formed an early globalised system of trade and diplomacy.",
      "In the 1300s BC, the eastern Mediterranean was surprisingly connected. Egypt, the Hittites in Anatolia, Babylon, Assyria, the Mycenaean Greeks and trading cities such as Ugarit on the Syrian coast exchanged goods, gifts and letters. Diplomatic letters found at Amarna in Egypt, mostly in Akkadian cuneiform, show kings calling one another 'brother' and haggling over gold, horses and royal marriages.\n\nPalaces at the centre of these kingdoms ran complex economies, recording grain, livestock and workers on clay tablets. Like modern globalised systems, this one depended on many parts working together at once."],
    ["Bronze needed the world", "Bronze needs tin, which was rare, so Bronze Age power rested on long and fragile trade routes.",
      "Bronze is copper mixed with a little tin, roughly one part in nine. Copper was fairly available, with Cyprus a major source. Tin was much rarer and often had to travel huge distances, possibly from Central Asia among other sources. The whole Bronze Age economy therefore depended on long trade routes.\n\nA shipwreck found off Uluburun in southern Turkey, from the late 1300s BC, shows how rich that trade was. It carried about ten tonnes of copper ingots and about a tonne of tin, along with glass, ivory, ebony, gold jewellery and goods from at least half a dozen cultures. Bronze, and power, rested on fragile networks."],
    ["Palaces fall, raiders arrive", "Around 1200 BC many great centres were destroyed, and Egyptian records blame the Sea Peoples, who were probably as much a symptom as a cause.",
      "In the decades around 1200 BC, things began to break. Mycenaean palaces such as Pylos burned. The Hittite capital Hattusa was abandoned and burned, and the Hittite kingdom disappeared. Ugarit was destroyed and never rebuilt. Not everywhere fell at once, but by about 1150 BC the old system had collapsed. Egypt survived, shrunken, and Assyria and Babylon endured in reduced form. In Greece, Linear B writing stopped and literacy was lost for centuries.\n\nRamesses III's temple at Medinet Habu records a victory over invaders from the sea, around 1177 BC. Carvings show warriors and families in ox carts, suggesting migration. Historians call them the Sea Peoples, and many now see them as a symptom as much as a cause."],
    ["A perfect storm", "No single cause explains the collapse; drought, famine, earthquakes, raids and broken trade combined, and shocks spread through a tightly connected system.",
      "Pollen, lake sediments and cave formations point to drier conditions in the eastern Mediterranean at this time. Texts mention grain shortages: the Hittites asked Egypt for grain, and one letter urgently asks Ugarit for ships to carry food. Some researchers also argue that earthquakes struck over several decades.\n\nAny one pressure might have been survivable. Together they strained palace economies that relied on food surplus. Historians such as Eric Cline, in his book '1177 BC', describe a 'perfect storm'. In a tightly connected system, a shock in one place spreads. If tin stopped arriving, bronze tools could not be made. If grain failed, cities went hungry. If palaces fell, the scribes, craftsmen and traders who depended on them scattered. This is called systems collapse."],
    ["What came after", "The collapse cleared space for iron, new states and the alphabet, but it showed that connected systems can fall together.",
      "The centuries that followed are sometimes called a dark age, partly because written records become scarce. Population fell and many settlements shrank. Yet the collapse also cleared space for new things.\n\nIron, made from widely available ore rather than imported tin, gradually came into wide use, giving the Iron Age its name. Smaller states emerged, including the Phoenician cities, the kingdoms of Israel and Judah, and new Aramaean states. Phoenician traders spread a simple alphabet of about two dozen letters, much easier to learn than cuneiform or hieroglyphs. The Greeks later adapted it, and the Latin letters you are reading now descend from it. Connected systems bring wealth, but they can also fall together."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the diplomatic letters found at Amarna reveal about the Late Bronze Age?", ["Each kingdom lived in isolation and avoided contact with its neighbours","Egypt alone controlled all trade and sent letters only to its own officials","Kingdoms communicated mainly through war rather than diplomacy","Kings across the region wrote to one another and traded gifts as part of a connected world"], 3, "The letters show kings addressing one another as 'brother', which reveals a linked, early globalised system."],
    [1, "Why did making bronze tie the ancient economy to long trade routes?", ["Tin was rare and often had to be brought from far away","Copper could only be mined in Central Asia","Bronze could only be cast in coastal cities","Ships were needed to carry the heavy furnaces"], 0, "Because bronze needs tin, and tin was scarce, whole kingdoms depended on distant supply chains."],
    [2, "How do many historians now see the Sea Peoples?", ["As the single invading army that conquered every kingdom","As a symptom of the collapse as much as its cause","As a myth invented by later Greek writers","As Egyptian allies who defended the coast"], 1, "Records suggest migrating groups, and no single invader could have toppled so many kingdoms."],
    [3, "Why did several pressures together do more damage than any one alone?", ["Each pressure cancelled out the effects of the others","Palace economies had large reserves that absorbed every shock","Shocks spread through tightly connected systems, so failures compounded","Only earthquakes caused real damage, and the rest were minor"], 2, "A shock in one place spreads: no tin, no bronze; no grain, hungry cities; fallen palaces, scattered specialists."],
    [4, "What was one lasting result of the collapse?", ["Bronze became more widely used than ever before","Cuneiform spread to replace all other writing","Large centralised empires quickly rebuilt the old palaces","Iron, which did not depend on imported tin, came into wide use"], 3, "Iron ore was widely available, so the loss of tin trade pushed societies toward a new metal and a new age."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A connected world collapsed within a few generations. Why is still debated."});

WB.L("antikythera-mechanism", "ancient", "advanced", "The Antikythera mechanism: an ancient computer",
  "Divers searching for sponges found a lump of corroded bronze in a Roman-era shipwreck. More than a century later, scientists realised it was a geared machine that modelled the heavens.",
  [
    ["Treasure from the deep", "Sponge divers found a Roman-era shipwreck in 1900, and a corroded lump from it turned out to contain a gear wheel.",
      "In 1900, Greek sponge divers sheltering from a storm near the island of Antikythera, between Crete and the Greek mainland, found an ancient shipwreck. Over the next year, with only basic diving gear, they and the Greek government recovered bronze and marble statues, glassware, coins and pottery. The ship sank in the first century BC, probably carrying luxury goods towards Italy.\n\nAmong the finds were unremarkable lumps of corroded bronze and wood. In 1902 the archaeologist Valerios Stais noticed a gear wheel embedded in one. Few people could believe the ancient Greeks had built anything so intricate, and for decades the object remained a puzzle in an Athens museum."],
    ["Gears hidden in rock", "Modern X-ray scanning revealed dozens of tiny gears and thousands of inscribed letters that work like a user manual.",
      "The mechanism survives in 82 fragments, the largest about the size of a hand. Inside are at least 30 interlocking bronze gears, some with teeth only a millimetre or two apart.\n\nIn the 1950s the British physicist and historian Derek de Solla Price began studying it seriously. With X-ray images in the 1970s, he proposed that it was a calendar computer. Then, in 2005, the Antikythera Mechanism Research Project used high-resolution X-ray CT scanning and advanced surface imaging. These revealed hidden gears and thousands of tiny inscribed letters, many unseen for two thousand years. The inscriptions were a kind of user manual, describing what the dials showed and how to read them."],
    ["A hand-cranked cosmos", "Turning a handle moved pointers for the sun, moon and calendar, and an off-centre gear pair modelled the moon's changing speed.",
      "The mechanism sat in a wooden box about the size of a shoebox, with dials front and back. A user turned a handle on the side, moving the date forward or backward, and the gears carried the motion to the pointers. The front showed the zodiac and a 365-day calendar, with pointers for the sun and moon. A small ball, half pale and half dark, rotated to show the moon's phase. Researchers believe it also showed the five planets known to the Greeks.\n\nThe moon does not move at a steady speed: it moves faster when closer to Earth. Two slightly off-centre gears, linked by a pin sliding in a slot, made its pointer speed up and slow down. That is astronomy and engineering in a few centimetres of bronze."],
    ["Predicting eclipses", "Spiral dials on the back used long astronomical cycles to forecast eclipses, and another tracked the Olympic Games.",
      "The back held two large spiral dials. One followed the Metonic cycle: 19 solar years almost exactly match 235 lunar months, which helped keep lunar and solar calendars in step. The other tracked the Saros cycle of 223 lunar months, after which similar eclipses tend to recur. Inscriptions marked months when solar or lunar eclipses could be expected, and some even noted the likely time of day.\n\nThese cycles were known from centuries of Babylonian astronomical records. The Greeks combined that knowledge with their own geometric models of the heavens and turned the results into gears, so a user could predict eclipses years ahead.\n\nA small dial tracked a four-year cycle of major Panhellenic games, including the Olympics, so the device served everyday life too."],
    ["A lost technology", "No comparable geared device survives from antiquity, and nothing similar is known for more than a thousand years.",
      "Who made it is still debated. Estimates range across the second and early first centuries BC, and the calendar's month names point to Corinthian colonies. Some connect it to Archimedes of Syracuse, but there is no proof.\n\nThe most startling thing is that it is unique. Nothing of similar complexity survives from the ancient world, and nothing like it is known until astronomical clocks appeared in medieval Europe more than a thousand years later. Cicero mentions devices showing the heavens, so it was probably not the only one. Bronze was often melted down, which may explain why others did not survive. In 2021, a University College London team published a new reconstruction of the front display. The fragments are in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How was the mechanism's significance first hinted at?", ["It was found sitting on display in a Roman temple","A gear wheel was noticed embedded in a corroded lump from the wreck","Divers read an inscription explaining how it worked","It was recovered intact with its wooden box and all dials working"], 1, "The visible gear wheel suggested something far more intricate than ancient people were assumed to have built."],
    [1, "What did modern X-ray CT scanning reveal about the mechanism?", ["That it was a modern hoax added to the wreck","That it was a simple sundial with no moving parts","Hidden gears and thousands of tiny letters that act like a user manual","That it was made mostly of iron and stone"], 2, "The scans exposed internal gears and inscriptions describing what the dials showed and how to read them."],
    [2, "Why did the designers use two off-centre gears linked by a pin and slot?", ["To keep the sun's pointer moving at a perfectly steady rate","To stop the crank from turning backwards","To drive the Olympic Games dial on the back","To make the moon's pointer speed up and slow down like the real moon"], 3, "The moon moves faster when closer to Earth, and the gear pair reproduced that uneven motion."],
    [3, "What did the Saros cycle dial on the back help a user do?", ["Predict when similar eclipses would recur","Calculate the date of the next Olympic Games","Match the lunar calendar to the solar year","Show where the five planets would appear"], 0, "The 223-month Saros cycle tends to bring similar eclipses back, so the dial forecast them years ahead."],
    [4, "Why is the mechanism considered so remarkable?", ["It is the only device the ancient Greeks ever built, and no other is mentioned","Nothing as complex survives until medieval astronomical clocks, over a thousand years later","It proves the Greeks invented the Olympic Games, which it was built to track","Its creator is known and thoroughly documented in surviving Greek records"], 1, "It is unique among surviving ancient objects, though writers like Cicero suggest others once existed."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A lump of corroded bronze from a shipwreck held geared dials. What did they predict?"});

